1
00:00:01,198 --> 00:00:05,120
一英亩土地宽66英尺，长660英尺。
An acre of land is 66 feet across, 660 feet long.

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:08,036
它是衡量在这片土地上发生的一切事情的单位。
It is the unit by which all things that happen on it are measured.

3
00:00:08,334 --> 00:00:14,468
一个农民种玉米每英亩可能赚1200美元，如果改养牛，在丰收年每英亩产值可能达到3000美元。
A farmer might make $1,200 per acre of farming corn, raise cattle instead, and one acre may produce $3,000 in a good year.

4
00:00:14,798 --> 00:00:18,148
把它租给想在那儿建风车的人，能租到5000美元。
Rent it out to someone who wants to build a windmill on it, $5,000.

5
00:00:18,478 --> 00:00:23,040
但如果你选择把这块地用来建AI数据中心，那数字就会大幅飙升。
But choose to use that acre instead for an AI data center, that number goes way up.

6
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,124
每年大约有1000万美元。
About $10 million per year.

7
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:33,092
直到最近，这个数字只有在New York、Paris、
Tokyo、London这些最顶尖城市的黄金地段才能出现。
A number that until very recently could only be claimed by the land in
prestigious areas of the most prestigious cities New York, Paris, Tokyo, London.

8
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:37,920
直到“AI”这个词被放在“数据中心”前面时，情况发生了转变。
It wasn't until AI was placed in front of the words data center that a shift occurred.

9
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:46,560
这种转变只用了短短三年，而且变化极快，让数据中心从没人在意的东西变成了完全不同的东西。
A shift that has taken just three short, fast-moving years, moving data centers
from something nobody gave a passing thought about to something else entirely.

10
00:00:47,054 --> 00:00:49,520
它比电力或火更深层的东西。
Something more profound than electricity or fire.

11
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,680
算力将成为未来的货币。
Compute is going to be the currency of the future.

12
00:00:52,090 --> 00:00:56,070
Meta将投入数千亿美元用于算力的建设。
Meta will invest hundreds of billions of dollars into compute build-out.

13
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,800
我们称之为工厂的基础设施，也就是AI工厂。
Infrastructure we call factories, AI factories.

14
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:02,480
这是历史上迄今为止最大的AI基础设施项目。
The largest AI infrastructure project by far in history.

15
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:05,956
巨大的数据中心，规模极其庞大。
Colossal data centers, very, very massive structures.

16
00:01:06,606 --> 00:01:09,668
桥梁、发电厂、摩天大楼、体育场、码头、铁路。
Bridges, power plants, skyscrapers, stadiums, docks, railroads.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:11,360
在美国漫长的历史中，
In the long history of the U.S.

18
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:16,932
在建造重大工程时，只有两个项目引发了与现在AI数据中心类似的强烈抵制。
building major things, just two come anywhere close to the backlash being felt by AI data centers right now.

19
00:01:17,230 --> 00:01:18,000
一个是美国
There was the U.S.

20
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:26,148
在1950年代建设州际公路系统，当时人们激烈争论这些新铺设的道路到底要在哪些土地上铺设，
以及它们离人们日常生活有多近。
interstate system in the 1950s, a major fight over what land exactly all this new pavement would be poured over,
how close they would be to people's everyday life.

21
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:29,316
人们真的不想让高速公路穿过他们的社区。
People really didn't want a highway running through their neighborhood.

22
00:01:29,678 --> 00:01:37,444
另一个是1970年代的核能时代，每当有地方想动工建
设新发电厂时，就会爆发大规模抗议，导致工程停滞。
Then there was the nuclear decade of the 1970s, grinding to a halt as major
protests erupted at every site that wanted to break ground on a new plant.

23
00:01:37,806 --> 00:01:39,908
人们害怕核电站可能会害死他们。
People were scared nuclear plants might kill them.

24
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:44,400
在寻找能源替代方案时，核裂变必须被阻止。
Looking at the alternatives for energy, nuclear fission must be stopped.

25
00:01:44,686 --> 00:01:53,476
如果你今天去问一千个人，说不想在身边建数据中心的人数，
远多于70年代说不想在身边建核电站的人数。
Go out and ask a thousand people today, and the amount of people who say they don't want a data center next to them is far more than
the number who said they didn't want a nuclear plant next to them back in the 70s.

26
00:01:53,902 --> 00:01:58,400
那是学校里在国家测试越来越大核武器时的“钻入掩护”演习。
This was duck and cover drills in school countries testing ever bigger nuclear weapons.

27
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:03,680
为什么核电站在那个时刻比现在的数据中心更受欢迎呢？
How were nuclear plants more popular at that moment in time than data centers are now?

28
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:07,108
这场关于数据中心的争论到底在争什么？
What exactly is this fight over data centers actually about?

29
00:02:09,997 --> 00:02:12,164
本视频由Zogtalk赞助。
This video is sponsored by Zogtalk.

30
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:13,092
稍后会详细介绍。
More on them later.

31
00:02:16,302 --> 00:02:19,200
互联网曾有一条规则，而这条规则本身就是个问题。
The internet had a rule, and the rule was a problem.

32
00:02:19,534 --> 00:02:23,760
互联网的主干网，也就是城市之间的长距离电缆，是由政府拥有并出资建设的。
The backbone of the internet, the long wires between cities, were owned and paid for by the government.

33
00:02:24,014 --> 00:02:26,880
这些线路是供大学和实验室使用的，你不被允许用它们来做生意。
Meant for universities and labs, you weren't allowed to use them for business.

34
00:02:27,150 --> 00:02:30,320
你可以用它发送教授的邮件，但不能用它发送客户的邮件。
You could send a professor's email across it, you could not send a customer.

35
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:36,228
不允许任何商业活动，这原本没问题，直到互联网上出现了想要成为客户的人。
Zero commercial activity allowed, which was fine, until people showed up on the internet who wanted to be customers.

36
00:02:36,430 --> 00:02:39,588
接着，有几家公司，准确说是八家公司，出现了，他们想要服务这些客户。
And a few businesses, eight to be exact, showed up who wanted to serve them.

37
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:45,796
如果其中一家公司的客户想给另一家公司的客户发邮件，
他们之间唯一的线路就是他们不被允许使用的那条。
If a customer of one wanted to email a customer of the other, the only wire between them was the one they weren't allowed to use.

38
00:02:46,190 --> 00:02:49,956
所以这两家公司必须在某个地方把他们的电缆物理连接在一起。
So the two companies had to physically touch their wires together somewhere.

39
00:02:50,350 --> 00:02:54,560
一根线从一栋楼里出来，另一根线从另一栋楼里出来，然后在某个地方汇合。
A line coming out of one building, a line coming out of the other, and physically meeting somewhere.

40
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:56,640
这是两个客户能够交流的唯一办法。
That was the only way two customers could talk.

41
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:59,172
想想他们是怎么解决这个问题的，简直有点疯狂。
It's kind of crazy to think about how they solved it.

42
00:02:59,502 --> 00:03:09,508
就只是两个家伙，分别来自这最初的两家公司，他们从街上拉出两根线，每家公司一根，
然后拉进第一个汇合点，那只是位于 Virginia Tyson's Corner 一栋建筑里的普通办公室套间。
Just a couple of guys, one from each of these first companies, ran a wire from the street, one for each company,
and into the first meeting place, which was just a plain office suite in a building in Tyson's Corner, Virginia.

43
00:03:09,902 --> 00:03:11,920
起初是两家公司，然后又增加了一家，接着又是一家。
Two companies at first, then another and another.

44
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:17,200
两个月内，八个办公室连接在了一起，每家公司都从自己的建筑里拉出一根线进入这个房间。
Within two months, eight offices were connected together, each company running a line from their building and into this room.

45
00:03:17,454 --> 00:03:21,700
一旦更多公司加入了这个塞满电缆的房间，他们就搬到了马路对面的一个停车场。
Once more companies joined in that room filled with cables, they moved across the street to a parking garage.

46
00:03:21,998 --> 00:03:25,572
准确地说，是 Boone Boulevard 上一栋办公楼停车场旁边的一个小房间。
Actually, a tiny room off the garage of an office building on Boone Boulevard.

47
00:03:25,838 --> 00:03:27,120
一台服务器，一个插座。
One server, one plug.

48
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:27,600
就这么简单。
That was it.

49
00:03:27,726 --> 00:03:29,412
没人在那里工作，也没有任何计算任务在里面运行。
Nobody worked there, nothing was computed there.

50
00:03:29,614 --> 00:03:32,420
那只是为了让少数几家公司把他们的电缆连接在一起的地方。
It was a place for a handful of companies to touch their wires together.

51
00:03:33,006 --> 00:03:35,332
是的，我又说了一遍，我可能还要说第四遍。
Yes, I said it again, and I'll probably say it a fourth time.

52
00:03:35,886 --> 00:03:38,884
这是第一个公共互联网，也是世界上第一个数据中心。
This was the first public internet and the world's first data center.

53
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:43,108
这个房间存在的唯一目的，就是作为周围其他建筑连接的汇合点。
A room whose whole point was simply a meeting place for other buildings around it to touch.

54
00:03:43,502 --> 00:03:53,680
等到政府切断那些不被允许使用的线路时，全球大约一半的互联网流量已经通过了 Virginia
Tyson's Corner 那个停车场里的那个小房间。
By the time the government shut off the wire companies weren't allowed to use, around half the world's internet traffic was already
passing through that little room in that garage in Tyson's Corner, Virginia.

55
00:03:54,094 --> 00:03:57,040
今天去看看 Tyson's Corner，它看起来就像美国任何一个普通的城镇。
Visit Tyson's Corner today and it will appear as any town USA.

56
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:05,680
没有任何迹象能提醒你，这个地方，这个停车场里的房间，
正是创造了距离这里 30 分钟车程外景象的火花。
There is nothing that would alert you to the fact that this place, this little room in a garage,
was the spark that created what is 30 minutes down the road.

57
00:04:05,934 --> 00:04:09,572
那是世界上见过的最大规模的数据中心集群，也就是「数据中心走廊」。
The largest collection of data centers the world has ever seen, data center alley.

58
00:04:09,966 --> 00:04:12,228
一个接一个的数据中心。
Data center after data center after data center.

59
00:04:12,558 --> 00:04:16,067
想象一下 Super Walmart，所有的货架，从一端走到另一端需要花多少时间。
Picture Super Walmart, all the aisles, how much time it takes to walk from one end to the other.

60
00:04:16,269 --> 00:04:25,444
在这 20 英里的区域内，有相当于 250 个 Super Walmart 的数据中心，
拥有 5200 万平方英尺的地面空间，让服务器让数百万家公司把他们的电缆连接在一起。
In this 20 mile area, there are 250 Super Walmarts worth of data centers, 52 million square feet of floor space where servers let
millions of companies touch their wires together.

61
00:04:25,838 --> 00:04:27,520
重心已经形成了。
The center of gravity had formed.

62
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:33,764
有一种感觉，而且是强烈的感觉，就是身处这个全球公司互相交换信息的枢纽附近。
There was a sense, a lot of it, in being near the hub where every company around the world was exchanging information with each other.

63
00:04:35,694 --> 00:04:38,544
Google 说，普通人不会等待超过半秒。
The average person will not wait more than half a second, Google said.

64
00:04:38,624 --> 00:04:42,464
所以，如果你想让我们向用户展示你的广告，你就必须位于这个区域。
So if you want us to show people your ads, you must be located in this area.

65
00:04:42,798 --> 00:04:48,644
当用户进入一个带有广告的网站时，我们会发出广播，并提供我们认为该用户身份的画像。
When a person lands on a website with ads on it, we'll send out a broadcast and a profile of who we think this person on the site is.

66
00:04:49,038 --> 00:04:55,300
你有 100 毫秒的时间来分析这个画像，并告诉我们你愿意花多少钱让我们把广告展示给他们看。
You have 100 milliseconds to analyze that profile and tell us how
much you're willing to spend to have us put your ad in front of them.

67
00:04:55,726 --> 00:05:00,164
在 San Francisco 接收到广播需要 35 毫秒，或者在等待它到达的过程中就被消耗掉了。
Receive the broadcast in San Francisco in 35 milliseconds or eaten up, waiting for it to arrive.

68
00:05:00,686 --> 00:05:02,884
然后再花 35 毫秒把它发回去。
Then another 35 milliseconds to send it back.

69
00:05:03,374 --> 00:05:07,620
在你甚至还没看清这个人可能是谁之前，就已经消耗了 70% 的时间。
70% of your time was gone before you had even looked at who the person might be.

70
00:05:08,046 --> 00:05:10,224
你要比其他人更好地分析画像。
Analyze the profile better than everyone else.

71
00:05:10,384 --> 00:05:11,504
这就是你赚钱的方式。
That was how you made money.

72
00:05:11,584 --> 00:05:12,804
你的竞价会更出色。
Your bids would be better.

73
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:16,784
每一毫秒的往返传输时间都至关重要。
Every millisecond of time spent sending things back and forth mattered a lot.

74
00:05:16,974 --> 00:05:24,452
所以，不仅有数十家公司把服务器放在和 Google 相同的区域，
他们甚至愿意多花钱把服务器放在和 Google 完全相同的建筑里。
So not only did dozens of companies put servers in the same area as Google,
they paid extra money to put their servers in the exact same building as Google.

75
00:05:24,846 --> 00:05:30,624
这就是我们使用的所有互联网免费服务的工作原理，
这也是地球上最激烈、吞吐量最高的算力事件。
This is how all the free stuff we use on the internet works, and it is the most intense, highest volume compute event on the planet.

76
00:05:30,926 --> 00:05:35,264
输入一个网站，按下回车键，Google 每天就会发起数十亿次的拍卖。
Type in a website, hit enter, and Google fires off an auction billions of times per day.

77
00:05:35,630 --> 00:05:41,828
Google 搜索是一个很棒的产品，比其他的都要好，
但正是这个拍卖机器让 Google 成为了今天的公司。
Google Search was a great product, better than the rest, but this auction machine is what made Google into the company it is today.

78
00:05:42,190 --> 00:05:50,704
对于这个以及许多类似的交互，一旦互联网真正开始腾飞，
选择一个别人都不在的地方就不是一个选项。
For this, and many other interactions like it, once the internet really started to take off,
it was not an option to be somewhere everyone else wasn't.

79
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:57,584
如果你听说过 Virginia 的 Loudoun County 是美国最富有的县，这就是他们所说的那个地方。
If you've heard of Loudoun County, Virginia, that it is the richest
county in the United States, this is the place they're talking about.

80
00:05:57,966 --> 00:06:00,100
现在，这里有三点很重要。
Now it is important to point out three things here.

81
00:06:00,864 --> 00:06:04,464
第一，这里的居民之所以富有，并不是因为这里有数据中心。
One, the population here is not rich because the data centers are here.

82
00:06:04,718 --> 00:06:06,224
它是华盛顿特区的郊区。
It's a suburb of Washington, DC.

83
00:06:06,542 --> 00:06:09,584
第二，数据中心之所以在这里，并不是因为这里的居民富有。
Number two, the data centers are not here because the population is rich.

84
00:06:09,774 --> 00:06:11,396
它们之所以在这里，是因为它们必须在这里。
They're here because they needed to be here.

85
00:06:11,630 --> 00:06:16,452
第三，这里的当地政府之所以富有，是因为他们对数据中心征收的税收。
Number three, the local government here is rich because of taxes they put on the data centers.

86
00:06:16,654 --> 00:06:18,596
这是三件不同的事情，以免产生混淆。
Three different things, just so there's no confusion.

87
00:06:19,344 --> 00:06:22,384
互联网上的各大巨头要么已经有了，要么现在就在这里的空间。
The who's who of the internet all had or have space here.

88
00:06:22,574 --> 00:06:24,304
AOL 就在几英里外的路程。
AOL was a few miles down the road.

89
00:06:24,590 --> 00:06:26,464
AWS 是在 2006 年从这里开始的。
AWS started here in 2006.

90
00:06:26,734 --> 00:06:32,864
你可以租用剩余算力的第一个区域就是 US East One，这是每个软件工程师都很清楚的事。
The first region you could rent spare compute from was US East One, something every software engineer knows very well.

91
00:06:33,024 --> 00:06:39,044
这是每个云服务提供商的默认设置，而且它们直奔
Northern Virginia，因为那是大家原本就在的地方。
It is the default setting for every cloud provider and it rails right to Northern Virginia because that's where everyone already was.

92
00:06:39,534 --> 00:06:42,304
没窗户，又长又矮，没人抗议。
Windowless, long, low, nobody protested.

93
00:06:42,384 --> 00:06:43,424
很少有人知道它们在那儿。
Few knew they were there.

94
00:06:43,598 --> 00:06:47,344
2015 年的数据中心里，已经存有你拍过的每一张照片。
A data center in 2015 had every photo you had ever taken already.

95
00:06:47,504 --> 00:06:52,064
Netflix 在全国各地的数十个数据中心里，都存有其库中每一部电影的副本。
Netflix keeps copies of every single movie in its library in dozens of data centers around the country.

96
00:06:52,462 --> 00:06:55,824
WhatsApp 的消息全都流经这些数据中心，就在互联网上。
WhatsApp messages all flow through these data centers are the internet.

97
00:06:56,078 --> 00:07:01,188
直到最近，它们吸引的注意力还跟隔壁存放草坪椅的仓库差不多。
And until very recently, they drew about as much attention as the warehouse next door holding lawn chairs.

98
00:07:01,646 --> 00:07:05,988
然后有人决定在里面放进大量完全不同类型的计算机。
Then someone decided to put a lot of a very different type of computer inside.

99
00:07:07,438 --> 00:07:08,004
那是闪烁了吗？
Is that blinking?

100
00:07:08,544 --> 00:07:08,864
没。
No.

101
00:07:09,198 --> 00:07:11,012
我以为我昨晚给它充过电了。
I thought I charged that last night.

102
00:07:12,398 --> 00:07:13,504
这得花多久？
How long is this going to take?

103
00:07:13,744 --> 00:07:15,140
16 16 瓦？
16 16 watts?

104
00:07:15,566 --> 00:07:16,228
瓦是什么？
What is a watt?

105
00:07:16,718 --> 00:07:17,904
你知道瓦是什么。
You know what a watt is.

106
00:07:24,526 --> 00:07:25,284
大概一小时。
About one hour.

107
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:27,108
行，没问题。
All right, we are good though.

108
00:07:27,982 --> 00:07:28,996
电池回来了。
Battery is back.

109
00:07:30,158 --> 00:07:30,944
没闪烁。
No blinking.

110
00:07:31,344 --> 00:07:31,588
漂亮。
Nice.

111
00:07:32,334 --> 00:07:32,624
好的。
Okay.

112
00:07:33,198 --> 00:07:33,904
所以把你的手机插上。
So plug in your phone.

113
00:07:34,158 --> 00:07:34,944
大概是 10 瓦。
It's about 10 watts.

114
00:07:35,310 --> 00:07:36,740
打开电视，100 瓦。
Click the TV on 100 watts.

115
00:07:37,102 --> 00:07:38,624
打开微波炉，1000 瓦。
Turn on the microwave, 1000 watts.

116
00:07:38,864 --> 00:07:45,984
