00:15:05,664 --> 00:15:18,564
抗议接踵而至，从一个地方跳到下一个地方，向市长和政客施压要求拒绝。
从法官裁决到动工花了40年，最终在2026年4月动工，选址是在一家混凝土厂旁边的土地。
Protest after protest hopping from one place to the next, pressuring the mayor and politicians to say no.
40 years to get from judge to breaking ground, which finally happened April 2026, on a section of land next to a concrete plant.

228
00:15:18,862 --> 00:15:27,824
垃圾填埋场、机场、电线、高速公路，许多我们都在间接使用或受益的东西，
正是那些绝对不能建在我家附近的设施。
Landfills, airports, power lines, highways, many of the things that we all use or benefit
from indirectly are exactly the things that absolutely no way do not build that near me.

229
00:15:28,064 --> 00:15:39,012
它们没通过「餐桌测试」，也就是你和家人坐下来，讨论他们想建在就在那儿的垃圾填埋场，
你们都承认，是的，我们的垃圾会去那儿，而且每个人的垃圾都得有个去处。
They don't pass the kitchen table test, where you sit down with your family and you talk about the new landfill that they want to put
in right over there, and you all acknowledge that yes, our garbage will go there and everyone's garbage needs to go somewhere.

230
00:15:39,438 --> 00:15:41,424
我们的土地价值可能会下降。
The value of our land will probably go down.

231
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:41,680
就在那儿。
there.

232
00:15:41,838 --> 00:15:43,620
大概会产生交通问题，甚至可能有异味。
It's probably going to be traffic, maybe even a smell.

233
00:15:44,078 --> 00:15:49,584
尽管知道这是成千上万其他人都会使用的设施，但拒绝它还是那么容易。
Despite knowing it's something thousands of other people will use, it's so easy to hit no on that.

234
00:15:49,806 --> 00:15:53,060
数据中心也没通过「餐桌测试」，但不管怎样。
Data centers do not pass the kitchen table test either, yet anyway.

235
00:15:53,294 --> 00:15:56,324
但原因是大多数人还没真正考虑过，或者不想用语言表达出来的。
But for a reason most people haven't really considered or don't want to put into words.

236
00:15:56,814 --> 00:15:59,424
州监狱的所有好处，全留在州内。
All the benefits of a state prison, it all stays in the state.

237
00:15:59,584 --> 00:16:04,196
来自你周边地区的坏人被带走关押在里面，明确是为了不让他们伤害你。
Bad people from your immediate area are taken away and put inside explicitly so they cannot harm you.

238
00:16:04,430 --> 00:16:07,812
这是住在监狱旁边的人能清楚看到的公共服务。
That is a public service the person who lives next to the prison can clearly see.

239
00:16:08,144 --> 00:16:15,012
即使他们不喜欢法律，或者不喜欢法律系统的运作方式，甚至不喜欢监狱的存在，
但监狱的建立就是为了服务他们。
Even if they don't love the laws or how the legal system works, or
even that the prison is there, the prison it is built to serve them.

240
00:16:15,424 --> 00:16:16,864
就问它点什么。
Just ask it something.

241
00:16:17,134 --> 00:16:19,876
就像你跟人说话一样，就像你跟我说话一样。
Like you're speaking to a person, just like you're speaking to me.

242
00:16:20,878 --> 00:16:21,444
比如什么？
Like what?

243
00:16:22,062 --> 00:16:22,896
好，好。
Okay, okay.

244
00:16:23,150 --> 00:16:24,496
数据中心是做什么的？
What does a data center do?

245
00:16:24,976 --> 00:16:26,336
大概每两秒钟一次。
Every two seconds, roughly.

246
00:16:26,606 --> 00:16:29,056
这取决于你具体问什么，以及你在做什么。
Depends on exactly what you're asking and what you're doing.

247
00:16:29,390 --> 00:16:35,136
有一百万人能够向正在建设的巨型AI数据中心发送信息，它会填补空白并把信息发回去。
One million people are able to send something to one of the mega AI
data centers being built, and it will fill the void and send it back.

248
00:16:35,376 --> 00:16:36,644
「回去」是指任何地方。
Back is wherever.

249
00:16:36,974 --> 00:16:40,576
虽然有极小的概率是距离数据中心一英里外的房子，但大概不是。
There's a small chance it's the house a mile away from the data center, but probably not.

250
00:16:40,736 --> 00:16:42,736
在其中一些城镇里，只有一千人。
There are a thousand people in some of those towns.

251
00:16:42,896 --> 00:16:44,196
他们中的大多数甚至不用AI。
Most of them don't even use AI.

252
00:16:44,846 --> 00:16:47,136
这是一个更难的餐桌测试。
This is a much harder kitchen table test.

253
00:16:47,216 --> 00:16:51,616
当地人为了监狱建址而进行的争斗非常激烈，但它是诚实的，而且容易理解。
A local fight over where the prison should go, brutal, but it's honest and easy to understand.

254
00:16:51,822 --> 00:16:56,676
数据中心打破了那种「这是我们的烂摊子，所以这是我们的垃圾场」的旧道德几何。
A data center breaks the old moral geometry of this is our mess, so this is our dump.

255
00:16:57,134 --> 00:16:58,976
数据中心并不与其用户匹配。
A data center is not matched to its users.

256
00:16:59,056 --> 00:17:01,924
它与四个输入项匹配：电力、水、光纤和一笔交易。
It is matched to four inputs: power, water, fiber, and a deal.

257
00:17:02,222 --> 00:17:06,096
关于那笔交易的细节，餐桌就是达成那笔交易的地方。
That deal part, the details of it, the kitchen table is where that deal is made.

258
00:17:06,190 --> 00:17:09,284
而周围的人只关注两件事。
And the people around it are focused on exactly two things.

259
00:17:09,776 --> 00:17:13,856
这是Georgia州Morgan County目前的饮用水。
This is the current drinking water in Morgan County, Georgia.

260
00:17:14,126 --> 00:17:19,588
研究人员表示，在炎热的日子里，数据中心消耗的水量可能高达数百万加仑。
Researchers say the amount of water used by data centers can be up to millions of gallons during hot days.

261
00:17:20,012 --> 00:17:24,336
这房子建于2005年，而我们井里的水位下降了15英尺。
The house was built in 2005, and the water level in our wells dropped 15 feet.

262
00:17:24,622 --> 00:17:28,290
排在列表第一位之后紧接着下降的东西，就是第二位，水。
The thing falling just behind first on that list, number two, water.

263
00:17:28,622 --> 00:17:30,016
数据中心会发热。
Data centers get hot.

264
00:17:30,318 --> 00:17:32,976
从历史上看，最便宜的冷却方式和我们人类一样。
Historically, the cheap way to cool it is the same way we as humans do.

265
00:17:33,166 --> 00:17:39,908
你流汗排热，把水引入建筑，把芯片产生的热量传给水，让水蒸发，热量随之散去。
You sweat it out, put water into the building, put the heat that comes out of
the chips into that water, let the water evaporate, the heat leaves with it.

266
00:17:40,334 --> 00:17:46,020
全天运行这个循环，一个运行特定冷却系统的巨型数据中心可能会消耗一百万加仑的水。
Running that loop all day, a big data center running a particular type of cooling system can go through a million gallons.

267
00:17:46,222 --> 00:17:51,216
如果你把那个数据中心建在一个公用事业规模较小的干旱县，这个数字会让邻居们提出质疑。
If you put that data center in a dry county with a small utility, that is a number the neighbors are going to question.

268
00:17:51,374 --> 00:17:55,812
如果他们得不到清晰的答复，那可能是更大的问题，就像在Oregon的这个站点一样。
And if they can't get a clean answer, that might be the bigger issue, like at this site in Oregon.

269
00:17:56,016 --> 00:17:59,044
Google在那个城市消耗了超过四分之一的城镇用水量。
Google was using more than a quarter of the town's water in the city.

270
00:17:59,310 --> 00:18:01,988
甚至不是Google，他们甚至去法院起诉为了把这件事保密。
It wasn't even Google went to court to keep that a secret.

271
00:18:02,222 --> 00:18:04,416
这才是人们真正愤怒的地方。
That's the part people are actually mad about.

272
00:18:04,496 --> 00:18:08,388
看起来，这与其说是关于加仑数，不如说是关于没被告知。
It appears to be much less about the gallons, much more about not being told.

273
00:18:08,814 --> 00:18:15,140
如果你退后一步，看看家里洗澡、洗碗、喝水等活动的水量消耗，情况就是这样分布的。
If you back out and look at water usage by activities that are in a house
taking showers, doing dishes, drinking it, this is how it breaks down.

274
00:18:15,694 --> 00:18:17,700
农业是美国最大的耗水行业。
Farms are the biggest consumers of water in the US.

275
00:18:18,158 --> 00:18:24,228
杏仁、开心果、高尔夫球场排在前列，而角落里这个小方块就是数据中心。
Almonds, pistachios, golf courses rank up there, and this little box down in the corner is data centers.

276
00:18:24,782 --> 00:18:32,290
这并不意味着你可以把数据中心建在任何地方，也不意味着你可以肆意妄为，
但实际上有记录的对居民造成的伤害，也就是因为数
Doesn't mean you can put a data center anywhere, It doesn't mean you can be reckless, but actual documented harm to residents,
meaning someone lost water or had it degraded because of a data center, is a very short list.

277
00:18:32,290 --> 00:18:35,460
据中心导致有人缺水或水质变差的情况，记录非常少。
Doesn't mean you can put a data center anywhere, It doesn't mean you can be reckless, but actual documented harm to residents,
meaning someone lost water or had it degraded because of a data center, is a very short list.

278
00:18:35,918 --> 00:18:40,676
在 5400 个数据中心中，反复被提及的案例只有那几个。
Of the 5,400 data centers, the cases that get named over and over are the same handful.

279
00:18:41,358 --> 00:18:44,516
流传最广的一个是 Georgia 的 Newton County，关于水变浑浊的问题。
The one that goes around is Newton County, Georgia, about muddy-colored water.

280
00:18:44,750 --> 00:18:47,536
那不是大家使用的城市自来水，而是四个私人水井。
It's not the city water everyone uses, it's four private wells.

281
00:18:47,616 --> 00:18:56,496
而且为了全面了解情况，包括县委员在内的各方都表示，浑浊的水早在很多年前就出现了，
比 Meta 开始建造数据中心还要早很多年。
And by all counts, just to get all sides of the situation, including the county commissioner,
the cloudy water appeared many years ago, years before Metas started building its data center.

282
00:18:56,686 --> 00:19:00,976
但无论如何，这个行业看到的民调结果和大家看到的一样。
But either way, the industry reads the same polls everyone else does.

283
00:19:01,198 --> 00:19:05,536
新建筑的设计是采用闭环系统，这意味着它们会循环使用同样的水长达 10 年。
New buildings are being designed to run closed loop, which means they use the same water for 10 years.

284
00:19:05,696 --> 00:19:08,256
水只进入一次，然后就一直循环。
Water goes in one time and then it just recirculates.

285
00:19:08,416 --> 00:19:16,388
或者采用新型空气冷却系统，甚至完全不用水，因为在干旱地区，
如果新闻标题上写着消耗一百万加仑水，其代价将超过冷却节省下来的成本。
Or a new type of air design system or no water at all, because a million-gallon
headline in a dry county is going to cost more than cooling savings.

286
00:19:16,590 --> 00:19:25,536
但最核心的一点，也就是 77% 的人关注的焦点，是出现在
每一轮民调、每一轮民调、无论哪个政党中的那个数字。
But the number one thing, what 77% of people are focused on, it is the number
that shows up at the top of every poll, poll after poll, all political parties.

287
00:19:25,776 --> 00:19:31,172
大家在餐桌上讨论最多的问题就是：这会让我的电费上涨吗？
The number one thing being chatted about around the kitchen table is this going to make my electricity bill go up.

288
00:19:31,696 --> 00:19:36,576
宣布成立 Stargate，建设巨大的数据中心。
Announcing the formation of Stargate, the construction of colossal data centers.

289
00:19:36,816 --> 00:19:41,056
Nvidia 宣布将向 open AI 投资 1000 亿美元。
Nvidia announcing that it will invest $100 billion into open AI.

290
00:19:41,216 --> 00:19:43,940
那个项目将消耗大约 10 吉瓦的电力。
That project will use about 10 gigawatts of power.

291
00:19:44,558 --> 00:19:45,572
那是非常多的吉瓦。
That's a lot of gigawatts.

292
00:19:45,838 --> 00:19:48,900
1000 瓦，微波炉消耗的功率就是 1 千瓦。
1,000 watts, how much a microwave pulls is one kilowatt.

293
00:19:49,198 --> 00:19:52,676
1000 千瓦，也就是 1000 个微波炉，就是 1 兆瓦。
1,000 kilowatts, which is 1,000 microwaves, is one megawatt.

294
00:19:53,038 --> 00:19:54,656
所以理所当然地，我们继续这个规律。
So naturally we just keep the pattern going.

295
00:19:54,894 --> 00:19:58,372
1000 兆瓦，也就是 100 万个微波炉，就是 1 吉瓦。
1,000 megawatts, or what is 1 million microwaves, is 1 gigawatt.

296
00:19:58,862 --> 00:20:06,096
或者为了更具体一点，想象一下如果像 Chicago 这样的城市里，所有的家庭、公寓、住宅、
公寓楼，无论是什么，都在同一时间打开微波炉。
Or to make it a little more concrete, imagine if every home, apartment, house, condo,
whatever all turn their microwave on at the same time in a city like Chicago.

297
00:20:06,478 --> 00:20:07,300
那大约就是 1 吉瓦。
It's about 1 gigawatt.

298
00:20:07,822 --> 00:20:14,656
把所有数据中心提案加在一起，很容易就会开始怀疑：好吧，那确实是同时开启了大量的微波炉。
Add up all the data center proposals, and it's very easy to start wondering, all right,
that's a lot of microwaves being turned on at the same time.

299
00:20:14,736 --> 00:20:15,716
这些电从哪儿来？
Where is that gonna come from?

300
00:20:15,936 --> 00:20:19,776
在整个美国，今年能产生的电量是这么多。
Across the entire US, this is how much electricity can be generated this year.

301
00:20:19,982 --> 00:20:21,936
其中有一大部分不会被使用。
Of that, there's a decent amount that won't be used.

302
00:20:22,096 --> 00:20:22,532
就是盈余。
Surplus.

303
00:20:23,086 --> 00:20:25,216
到 2030 年，美国产生的盈余几乎不多了。
By 2030, the US barely generates more.

304
00:20:25,376 --> 00:20:26,244
不过仍然有盈余。
Still a surplus, though.

305
00:20:26,638 --> 00:20:31,364
到2035年，可用的电量将少于2030年，而且盈余也将消失。
By 2035, there's less electricity available than 2030, and the surplus disappears.

306
00:20:31,950 --> 00:20:34,576
那么这些电将从哪里来呢？
So where is this electricity going to come from?

307
00:20:34,816 --> 00:20:40,356
人们把电网当成州际公路系统，一个每个部分都与整体相连的互联网络。
People refer to the grid as though it's an interstate highway system, a connected web where each part connects to the whole.

308
00:20:40,814 --> 00:20:41,476
但事实并非如此。
It is not.

309
00:20:42,784 --> 00:20:50,564
美国全境，所有50个州，按理说应该是整齐划一的，但电
力系统并不是这样运作的，并不是每个州内部都整齐划一。
The United States of America, all 50 states, right as they should be,
but this is not how the electric system works, tidy inside of each state.

310
00:20:51,214 --> 00:20:51,652
而是这样。
This is.

311
00:20:52,174 --> 00:20:54,404
这就是美国的电力区域。
This is the electric states of America.

312
00:20:54,894 --> 00:21:00,548
当你打开微波炉时，没有任何东西能保证流进其中的电流是来自离你最近的发电厂。
When you turn your microwave on, there's nothing that says the
electricity that flows into it comes from the power plant closest to you.

313
00:21:01,006 --> 00:21:05,924
它可能是，也可能来自该用户居住的电力区域范围内的任何其他发电厂。
It might, or it might come from any of the other power plants inside the bounds of the electric state in which a person lives.

314
00:21:06,304 --> 00:21:14,090
就在你按下启动键的那一刻，某座发电厂正在运转并产生电力，
从每个区域的数百座发电厂中通过输电线路流出，进入
A plant that is fired up at that moment and making electricity when you hit start,
flowing out of any of the hundreds of plants in each zone through transmission

315
00:21:14,090 --> 00:21:14,190
就在你按下启动键的那一刻，某座发电厂正在运转并产生电力，
从每个区域的数百座发电厂中通过输电线路流出，进入
lines, into local lines, into your house, just as movie night is about to start.

316
00:21:14,190 --> 00:21:17,984
配电线路，进入你的房子，就在电影夜即将开始的时候。
lines, into local lines, into your house, just as movie night is about to start.

317
00:21:19,182 --> 00:21:22,212
随着19世纪电力的发展，发电厂规模较小且属于本地。
As electricity took off in the 1800s, power plants were small and local.

318
00:21:22,384 --> 00:21:24,452
它们将自己的线路连接到自己的客户身上。
They connected their own lines to their own customers.

319
00:21:24,782 --> 00:21:28,516
然而，发电厂开始变得越来越大，功率也越来越强。
Power plants, though, started to be made bigger and they were made more powerful.

320
00:21:28,814 --> 00:21:33,156
拥有几百座大型发电厂，而不是几千座小型发电厂，开始变得非常有意义。
It started making a lot of sense to have a few hundred big ones instead of thousands of smaller ones.

321
00:21:33,646 --> 00:21:38,756
因此，他们自然地开始将一组电线连接到另一组电线
上，小型公用事业公司和发电厂合并成更大的实体。
So they naturally started connecting one set of wires to another, small utilities and power plants combining to make a bigger one.

322
00:21:39,022 --> 00:21:39,824
这样更可靠。
It was more reliable.

323
00:21:39,950 --> 00:21:45,264
电力生产成本可以降低，就像在每个城镇都有一家生产汽油的公司是不合理的一样。
The electricity could be made cheaper, just like it wouldn't make sense to have one company that made gasoline in each town.

324
00:21:45,486 --> 00:21:48,384
相反，几家大型炼油厂为整个地区生产汽油。
Instead, a few giant refineries make gasoline for the whole region.

325
00:21:48,782 --> 00:21:50,784
实际上，汽油和柴油的情况正是如此。
Gasoline and diesel are exactly like this, actually.

326
00:21:50,944 --> 00:21:54,788
如果你看地图，石油储存和炼油的分布也是类似的奇怪地图。
If you look at the map, it's the same kind of weird map for oil storage and refining.

327
00:21:55,024 --> 00:22:00,196
正是这种奇怪的分布，才导致单纯看美国总共能生产多少电是没有意义的。
This weird map is why it doesn't make any sense to look at how much total electricity the US can make.

328
00:22:00,590 --> 00:22:05,444
重要的是每个区域能生产多少电，以及每个区域是供过于求还是供不应求。
What matters is how much each region can make and whether each region has more than enough or not enough.

329
00:22:06,062 --> 00:22:14,852
这就是事情变得有趣的地方，因为就像美国的50个独立州，每个都是独立的小实验一样，
美国的电力区域也是一个个实验。
And this is where things get interesting because just like the 50 individual states of the US, each one its own little experiment,
the electric states of America are also experiments.

330
00:22:15,184 --> 00:22:17,024
它们并不是都以同样的方式运作。
They do not all do things the same way.

331
00:22:17,390 --> 00:22:24,484
从如何决定建造更多发电厂、选择什么类型的发电厂，到我
们围坐在餐桌旁讨论的账单上包含什么内容，全都是不同的。
How decisions to build more power plants are made, what kinds of plants to have,
all the way to what to put on our bills that we discuss around the kitchen table.

332
00:22:24,942 --> 00:22:25,504
美国大部分地区
Most of the U.S.

333
00:22:25,678 --> 00:22:29,924
人口都集中在一个与拥有全球最多数据中心的区域相同的区域。
population is in a region just like the one that hosts the world's most data centers.

334
00:22:30,190 --> 00:22:32,196
没有任何一家公司拥有系统的所有部分。
No one company owns every part of the system.

335
00:22:32,622 --> 00:22:38,372
一家公司拥有发电厂，另一家拥有大型输电线路，还有一家拥有你家附近的线路并向你发送账单。
One company owns the power plant, another the big transmission lines,
another one owns the lines in your neighborhood and sends you your bill.

336
00:22:38,704 --> 00:22:48,068
而另一个组织负责协调所有这些公司合作，并运行数百场拍卖，决定你支付的电费价格。
And yet another organization is responsible for getting all of those companies to cooperate and to run hundreds of auctions that
decide the price you pay for your electricity.

337
00:22:48,622 --> 00:22:50,340
这些拍卖的运作方式是这样的。
Those auctions work like this.

338
00:22:50,734 --> 00:22:51,588
第一场拍卖。
Auction number one.

339
00:22:52,174 --> 00:22:56,784
每小时，每座电厂都会参加拍卖，报出他们出售电力的最低金额。
Every hour, every plant joins an auction saying the minimum amount of money they'll sell their power for.

340
00:22:57,230 --> 00:22:58,052
竞标开始。
Bids roll in.

341
00:22:58,222 --> 00:23:04,224
负责拍卖的机构会查看所有要求的电量，并根据历史模型预测下一小时的预期需求。
The organization running the auction looks at all the electricity being
asked for and what their historical models say to expect over the next hour.

342
00:23:04,526 --> 00:23:05,984
他们按金额对竞标进行排序。
They sort the bids by amount.

343
00:23:06,190 --> 00:23:10,404
最后一个能满足预期所需全部电量的报价，就是成交价格。
The last bid that covers all the power they expect to need, that is the clearing price.

344
00:23:10,670 --> 00:23:14,148
这个价格就成了所有提交了更低报价的电厂的结算价格。
And that price becomes the price for every power plant that submitted a lower bid.

345
00:23:14,446 --> 00:23:22,624
换句话说，如果我说 5 美元，你说 10 美元，而我们需要
你的电来满足交付给客户的量，那么你的报价就会被接受。
In other words, if I say $5 and you say $10 and we need your electricity
to cover the amount we need to deliver to customers, your bid is accepted.

346
00:23:22,894 --> 00:23:25,264
你拿到 10 美元，我也拿到 10 美元。
You get paid $10, I also get paid $10.

347
00:23:25,710 --> 00:23:26,304
第二场拍卖。
Auction two.

348
00:23:26,926 --> 00:23:31,012
同样类型的拍卖，但这是一场每年仅举行一次的重大拍卖。
Same type of auction, but this is a single major auction that happens once every year.

349
00:23:31,310 --> 00:23:32,644
它只问一个简单的问题。
Asking a simple question.

350
00:23:33,038 --> 00:23:41,584
“为了确保你在两年后需求最高的那个最热的夏天下午能提供电力，我们得付给你多少钱？”
What do we have to pay you to make sure you are available on the worst, hottest
summer afternoon when electricity demand is at its highest two years from now?

351
00:23:41,806 --> 00:23:47,268
拍卖方会查看他们认为需要的电量，这是一个展望两年的预测。
The people holding the auction look at how much electricity they think they'll need, a forecast looking two years into the future.

352
00:23:47,566 --> 00:23:48,464
“我们需要多少电？”
How much do we need?

353
00:23:48,624 --> 00:23:49,984
“我们认为需求会增长多少？”
How much do we think that will grow?

354
00:23:50,064 --> 00:23:51,424
“有多少电厂要退役？”
How many plants are going to retire?

355
00:23:51,504 --> 00:23:52,676
“有多少电厂将被新建？”
How many are going to be built?

356
00:23:53,070 --> 00:24:00,384
把这些数字加起来，那些能提供超过预测总量的电厂给出的价格，就是每家电厂获得的报酬。
Add up the numbers, and the price that comes in from the plant that gets
the total amount over the forecast, that's what every plant gets paid.

357
00:24:00,464 --> 00:24:02,596
这个金额会分摊到每个人的账单上。
And that amount is split across everyone's bill.

358
00:24:02,704 --> 00:24:07,264
这是一种保险支付，就是为了确保在最糟糕的日子里灯依然亮着。
It is an insurance payment, a just in case, to make sure the lights stay on on the worst day.

359
00:24:07,502 --> 00:24:10,340
但如果我们往南方走，情况就简单多了。
But if we mosey on down to the south, things get much easier.

360
00:24:10,606 --> 00:24:14,916
全美只有两个地区运行这种系统：东南部和西部的一大片区域。
Only two areas of the country live in this type of system the southeast and a big swath of the west.

361
00:24:15,406 --> 00:24:16,484
这些地区的模式要简单得多。
These areas are much simpler.

362
00:24:16,782 --> 00:24:22,544
一家公司，比如 Georgia 的 Southern Company，拥有
电厂、电线，并负责寄送账单，这就是所谓的垂直整合。
One company, like the Southern Company in Georgia, owns the plants,
the wires, and sends you the bill, what is called vertically integrated.

363
00:24:22,734 --> 00:24:28,464
他们每年发布一次大型预测，以确定明年为了让客户满意需要多少电量。
Once per year, they publish a big forecast to determine how much
electricity they'll need to keep their customers happy the following year.

364
00:24:28,942 --> 00:24:33,104
Southern 公司会去找 Georgia 政府说：“这是明年维持供电所需的成本。”
Southern goes to the Georgia government and says, here's what it's going to cost to keep the lights on next year.

365
00:24:33,326 --> 00:24:34,304
“请允许我们收取这个费用。”
Please let us charge that.

366
00:24:34,638 --> 00:24:39,904
委员会会审查一下，说同意、不同意，或者不能收这么多，
最终他们协商出的数字就是客户支付的金额。
A commission looks at it, says yes, no, or not that much, and whatever number they negotiate is what customers pay.

367
00:24:40,142 --> 00:24:45,220
所以没有每小时的拉锯战，没有两年的预留费，只有一家公司、一次听证、一次休会。
So no hourly dogfight, no two-year reservation fee, one company, one hearing, one break.

368
00:24:45,774 --> 00:24:49,104
不过再往西一点到 Texas，这个地区就很特殊。
A little farther west in Texas, though, this region is unique.

369
00:24:49,326 --> 00:24:52,164
在整个国家里，它是唯一像这样运作的地区。
In the entire country, it is the only region that works like this.

370
00:24:52,622 --> 00:24:53,504
简单到极点。
As simple as can be.

371
00:24:53,584 --> 00:24:58,144
有趣的是，注意这个地区的边界完全在州界之内。
And interestingly, note that the borders of this region are entirely within the state borders.

372
00:24:58,446 --> 00:25:02,944
这是一个非常刻意的决定，因为这是他们被允许按这种方式运作的唯一原因。
A very intentional decision because that is the only reason they're allowed to do it the way that they do.

373
00:25:03,104 --> 00:25:07,264
如果你听过「州际贸易」这个词，这就是 Texas 不想触碰的红线。
If you've ever heard the term interstate commerce, that is what Texas does not want to trip.

374
00:25:07,584 --> 00:25:14,944
一旦连接到为 Texas 以外客户服务的系统，联邦政府就有权对 Texas 如何运行其系统制定规则。
Connect to a system that serves customers outside of Texas, and the federal
government is then allowed to put rules on Texas about how it runs its system.

375
00:25:15,054 --> 00:25:17,104
而 Texas 并不想要那些规则。
And Texas does not want those rules.

376
00:25:17,264 --> 00:25:21,904
系统将变得比目前运作的模式复杂得多，目前的模式只是全天候的竞拍。
The system would become much more complicated than how it currently works, which is just a round-the-clock auction.

377
00:25:22,144 --> 00:25:24,356
「我们需要这么多电，你们卖多少钱？」
We need this much electricity, how much will you sell it for?

378
00:25:24,622 --> 00:25:26,784
而且电厂只有在实际发电时才会拿到报酬。
it And a plant only gets paid when it actually makes the power.

379
00:25:27,022 --> 00:25:27,424
就是这样。
That is it.

380
00:25:27,584 --> 00:25:28,624
大部分时间都很便宜。
Most hours that's cheap.

381
00:25:28,784 --> 00:25:37,504
但在一个酷热的下午，当达拉斯所有的空调都在全功率运转，
或者像几年前那样的极端冬季风暴时，那一小时的价格允许飙升到 5,000 美元。
On a brutal afternoon, when every air conditioner in Dallas is running full blast or a freak winter storm like a couple years ago,
the price of that hour is allowed to go as high as $5,000.

382
00:25:38,062 --> 00:25:40,224
总之，各地的情况并不都一样。
All that to say, it's not the same everywhere.

383
00:25:40,462 --> 00:25:43,460
三种不同的系统将导致至少有些不同的结果。
Three different systems will lead to three, at least somewhat different outcomes.

384
00:25:44,046 --> 00:25:48,964
这并不是说其中任何一种能完全解决电力需求，但这就是现状。
That's not to say any of them solves the demand for electricity entirely, but this is where things stand now.

385
00:25:49,390 --> 00:25:51,664
这是每个地区今年将拥有的盈余。
This is how much surplus each region will have this year.

386
00:25:51,854 --> 00:25:53,508
大家的盈余都处于不错的水平。
Everyone is in the green by a decent amount.

387
00:25:53,998 --> 00:25:56,964
快进到 2030 年，情况开始发生显著变化。
Fast forward to 2030, and the picture starts to shift significantly.

388
00:25:57,294 --> 00:26:01,444
那些流传着电费上涨传闻的市场，现在已经处于赤字状态。
The markets where stories of rising electric bills have swirled are already in deficit.

389
00:26:01,966 --> 00:26:06,944
再到 2035 年，那些今天电费上涨的区域，情况会急剧恶化。
Out to 2035, and those same areas with bill increases today, get dramatically worse.

390
00:26:07,260 --> 00:26:14,620
你的电费上涨，以及所有这些数据中心扮演的角色，Dominion 的客户是否应该继续分担这些成本？
Your rising power bill and how all these data centers are playing a role should Dominion customers continue sharing those costs.

391
00:26:14,704 --> 00:26:15,504
谁该为此买单？
Who should pay for it?

392
00:26:15,744 --> 00:26:17,184
必须由数据中心行业来承担。
It has to be the data center industry.

393
00:26:17,504 --> 00:26:23,744
面临风险的是每一个人，每个家庭都有可能面临支付金额翻倍甚至翻三倍的风险。
What's at risk is every person, every household runs the risk of a doubling or even a tripling of the amount they pay?

394
00:26:23,904 --> 00:26:24,996
并不是全部都是数据中心造成的。
Not all of it is data centers.

395
00:26:25,344 --> 00:26:31,332
有些是常规维护，有些是保养，还有些是错误，即那些两年的预测并没有实际发生。
Some of it is regular upkeep, some is maintenance, and some is an error where those two-year out projections didn't actually happen.

396
00:26:31,662 --> 00:26:35,748
负责竞拍的机构说会有 30 吉瓦的电力需求，但实际上只有 5 吉瓦。
The organization that holds the auction said 30 gigawatts were coming, but only five did.

397
00:26:36,078 --> 00:26:39,364
那项计算产生的费用已经出现在客户的账单上了。
The charge from that calculation already appeared on customers' bills.

398
00:26:39,664 --> 00:26:41,184
这就是反对它的理由。
So that's the case against it.

399
00:26:41,344 --> 00:26:50,280
如果有人出现在你的镇上，要求消耗足以供养一座城市的电量，
而电力公司需要投入数十亿美元建设新的电线、变电站和发电厂来满足他们，
If someone shows up in your town asking for enough electricity to power a city, and the power company needs billions of dollars of new
wires and substations and power plants to serve them, there's an extremely reasonable question to ask.

400
00:26:50,280 --> 00:26:52,452
那么有一个非常合理的问题该问。
If someone shows up in your town asking for enough electricity to power a city, and the power company needs billions of dollars of new
wires and substations and power plants to serve them, there's an extremely reasonable question to ask.

401
00:26:52,910 --> 00:26:53,956
这一切谁来买单？
Who pays for all of that?

402
00:26:54,286 --> 00:27:03,620
因为如果答案是你，如果这家公司决定在这里建设导致你餐桌上的账单数字上涨，
那么全球的AI野心突然就变成了你的电费。
Because if the answer is you, if this company's decision to build here makes the number on your kitchen table go up,
then suddenly the world's AI ambitions have become your electric bill.

403
00:27:04,558 --> 00:27:10,724
但这场争论还有另一面，因为对于一家公司出现并要求十亿瓦电力，有两种看待方式。
But there is another side of this fight, because there are two ways to
look at a company showing up and asking for a billion watts of electricity.

404
00:27:10,944 --> 00:27:14,820
一种是，我们到底还能从哪儿找到另外十亿瓦的电力？
One is, where are we possibly going to find another billion watts?

405
00:27:15,264 --> 00:27:18,980
另一种是，有人出现了，愿意买下这十亿瓦的电力。
The other is, someone just showed up offering to buy a billion watts.

406
00:27:19,310 --> 00:27:22,224
而这两种看法是对同一件事完全不同的解读。
And those are very different ways of looking at the exact same thing.

407
00:27:22,574 --> 00:27:29,744
在现代美国历史的大部分时间里，电力需求增长得足够缓慢，
缓慢到系统基本能随着需求同步缓慢增长。
For most of modern American history, electricity demand grew slowly
enough, so slowly that the system could mostly grow slowly with it.

408
00:27:29,966 --> 00:27:31,652
AI 彻底改变了这一点。
AI has definitively changed that.

409
00:27:31,904 --> 00:27:38,704
几代人以来第一次，巨大的客户出现了，他们说，如果你能制造出多得多的电力，我们就买。
For the first time in generations, enormous customers are showing up saying, if you can make vastly more electricity, we will buy it.

410
00:27:38,864 --> 00:27:39,904
我们要买很多。
We will buy a lot of it.

411
00:27:40,064 --> 00:27:43,364
不是买一小时，不是只在便宜的时候买，而是全天候，每一天都要买。
Not for an hour, not when it's cheap, all day, every single day.

412
00:27:43,630 --> 00:27:51,584
突然之间，我们刚才看到的这些奇特的电力状态——构建方式不同、监管不同、
定价也不同——都被扔进了同一个实验中。
And suddenly, all of these weird electric states we just looked at, systems built differently, regulated differently,
priced differently, have been dropped into the same experiment.

413
00:27:51,758 --> 00:27:52,304
你能盖房子吗？
Can you build?

414
00:27:52,622 --> 00:27:53,412
你能盖得快吗？
Can you build quickly?

415
00:27:53,678 --> 00:27:56,452
你能不让当地居民买单就能盖吗？
Can you build without making everyone already living there pay for it?

416
00:27:56,782 --> 00:27:59,524
如果你能做到，那个地方会变成什么样？
If you can, what happens to that place that does?

417
00:28:00,046 --> 00:28:09,860
你会变成一个能够生产大量可靠且廉价电力的地方，而现在的经济体系正日益渴求这种电力。
You become a place capable of producing enormous quantities of reliable, inexpensive electricity in an economy that increasingly wants
enormous quantities of reliable, inexpensive electricity.

418
00:28:10,158 --> 00:28:16,224
AI需要它，工厂需要它，先进制造业也需要它，甚至AI之后的任何技术可能也需要它。
AI needs that, factories need that, advanced manufacturing needs that, and whatever comes after AI may need it too.

419
00:28:16,384 --> 00:28:19,748
所以，也许这些数据中心不仅仅是在消耗电力系统。
So maybe these data centers aren't just consuming the electric system.

420
00:28:20,224 --> 00:28:24,740
也许它们是在押注哪些电力系统能够扩张。
Maybe they are placing an enormous bet on which electric systems can grow.

421
00:28:25,006 --> 00:28:28,944
现在，每一个电力州都必须决定是否要接受这个赌注。
And now every one of the electric states has to decide whether it wants to take that bet.

422
00:28:29,230 --> 00:28:35,264
所以，美国最大的四家公司今年正投入大约7000亿美元在这些建筑上，他们是在下注。
So the four biggest companies in America are spending, they're betting, something like $700 billion this year on these buildings.

423
00:28:35,424 --> 00:28:36,704
去年大约是4000亿美元。
Last year was about $400 billion.

424
00:28:36,864 --> 00:28:41,184
整个州际公路系统，按里程计算，如果考虑到通货膨胀，成本大约是5000亿美元。
The entire interstate highway system, every mile, if you adjust for inflation, costs about $500 billion.

425
00:28:41,504 --> 00:28:43,076
那是花了40年的时间才花出去的。
That was spent over 40 years.

426
00:28:43,278 --> 00:28:45,504
而这些公司在短短12个月内就花出了超过那个数额。
These companies are spending more than that in 12 months.

427
00:28:45,742 --> 00:28:51,904
还记得US East One吗？那是整个互联网的默认设置，
也是为什么这么多在线活动都要经过Virginia的原因。
Remember US East One, the default setting for the whole internet, the reason so much of what happens online passes through Virginia.

428
00:28:52,494 --> 00:29:01,632
现在，当伦敦、圣保罗或雅加达的某人要求 AI 来填补空白时，
这个空白极有可能是在美国的这里被填补的。
Right now, when someone in London or Sao Paulo or Jakarta asks an AI to fill the void,
there's a very good chance that void gets filled here in the United States.

429
00:29:01,792 --> 00:29:03,472
这就是论点的另一面。
And that is the other side of the argument.

430
00:29:03,632 --> 00:29:13,472
那些投入巨资的人相信，算力将成为全球经济中最重要的投入
之一，算力将成为一种出口，而且其重要性至少与石油相当。
The people spending all this money believe compute is going to become one of the most important inputs in the global economy,
that compute is going to become an export and one that is at least as important as oil.

431
00:29:13,712 --> 00:29:18,832
如果他们是对的，问题就不只是谁在用 AI，而是 AI 是在哪里制造出来的。
And if they're right, the question isn't just who uses the AI, it's where the AI gets made.

432
00:29:19,022 --> 00:29:24,644
因为当一个国家成为重要新技术的早期中心时，它捕捉到的不仅仅是技术本身。
Because when a country becomes the early center of an important new technology, it doesn't capture only the thing itself.

433
00:29:24,878 --> 00:29:32,420
资金会涌入，公司会涌入，供应商会涌入，工程师也会涌入，然后人们会思考还能造出什么，
因为所有这些基础都已经在那儿了。
The money comes, the companies come, the suppliers come, the engineers come, and then people figure out what else they can build
because all of those things are already there.

434
00:29:32,718 --> 00:29:35,268
事实上，我们已经在这一故事中看到过这种情况发生了一次。
We've actually watched this happen once already in this story.

435
00:29:35,552 --> 00:29:38,276
为什么所有人都把服务器放在北弗吉尼亚州？
Why did everybody put their servers in Northern Virginia?

436
00:29:38,542 --> 00:29:41,572
因为其他所有人已经把服务器放在北弗吉尼亚州了。
Because everybody else had already put their servers in Northern Virginia.

437
00:29:41,934 --> 00:29:46,112
重心形成了，然后这个重心就成了继续在那里建设的理由。
The center of gravity formed, and then the center of gravity became a reason to keep building there.

438
00:29:46,352 --> 00:29:50,832
建造这些设备的人认为，AI 可能会以同样的方式运作。
The argument from people building these boxes is that AI could work the same way.

439
00:29:50,992 --> 00:29:55,232
只不过现在的竞争不再是弗吉尼亚州和加利福尼亚州之间，而是国家之间的竞争。
Except now the competition isn't between Virginia and California, it's between countries.

440
00:29:55,472 --> 00:29:58,272
这张图表展示了美国目前的领先优势。
And this chart is what America's lead looks like today.

441
00:29:58,446 --> 00:30:00,772
领先优势非常显著，但并非不可逾越。
A formidable lead, but not insurmountable.

442
00:30:01,520 --> 00:30:02,432
美国，Loudoun County。
Loudoun County, U.S.

443
00:30:02,512 --> 00:30:05,412
East One 之所以富有，并不是因为那里有数据中心。
East One, it's not wealthy because the data centers are there.

444
00:30:05,646 --> 00:30:10,192
我们已经确定了这一点，但它的政府从这些中心那里获得了巨额资金。
We established that already, but its government has collected enormous amounts of money from them.

445
00:30:10,446 --> 00:30:16,164
这些资金帮助支付了非常优质的学校和公共服务，并让该县降低了近 40% 的财产税。
Money that has helped pay for very nice schools and public services and allowed the county to lower property taxes by almost 40%.

446
00:30:16,672 --> 00:30:18,032
所以，这就是他们的推销点。
So here is the pitch.

447
00:30:18,350 --> 00:30:21,956
再做一次，只不过规模更大，而且是针对整个美国。
Do that again, except bigger, except for the entire United States.

448
00:30:22,382 --> 00:30:29,232
如果世界要投入数万亿美元来建设人工智能背后的基础设施，那就把大部分基础设施建在这里。
If the world is going to spend trillions of dollars building the infrastructure
behind artificial intelligence, build a disproportionate amount of it here.

449
00:30:29,392 --> 00:30:37,952
在这里发电，在这里建设基础设施，在这里征税，在这里培养专业知识，
并希望在所有这些算力之上建立的任何东西，也都能建在这里。
Generate the electricity here, build the infrastructure here, collect the taxes here, develop the expertise here,
and hope that whatever gets built on top of all that compute gets built here too.

450
00:30:38,126 --> 00:30:38,832
这就是赌注。
That is the bet.

451
00:30:38,990 --> 00:30:40,132
这并不是一个荒谬的赌注。
And it's not a ridiculous one.

452
00:30:40,672 --> 00:30:42,512
但它有一个问题。
But there is a problem with it.

453
00:30:42,592 --> 00:30:46,272
因为坐在餐桌旁的那个人，并不是美利坚合众国。
Because the person sitting at the kitchen table, it's not the United States of America.

454
00:30:46,432 --> 00:30:48,752
他们是坐在餐桌旁的那个人。
They're the person sitting at the kitchen table.

455
00:30:49,102 --> 00:30:50,432
所以让我们把这个逻辑串联起来。
So let's bring this full circle.

456
00:30:50,638 --> 00:30:54,212
核电站是一座为周边居民提供电力的建筑。
A nuclear plant is a building that makes electricity for the people who live around it.

457
00:30:54,542 --> 00:31:00,192
数据中心则是为了代表大部分住在别处、甚至可能在很远地方的人，而消耗电力的建筑。
A data center is a building that uses electricity on behalf of people who mostly live somewhere else, probably far away.

458
00:31:00,590 --> 00:31:03,460
属于同类不受欢迎的东西，只是流向相反。
Same category of unwanted thing, opposite direction of flow.

459
00:31:03,918 --> 00:31:10,212
如果你正坐在餐桌旁，测试就在那儿结束了，还没等任何人开口讨论 AI 是什么或不是什么。
If you're sitting around the kitchen table, the test it ends right there, before anyone says a word about what AI is or is not.

460
00:31:10,606 --> 00:31:15,952
但与核电站的联系还不完整，因为抗议并不是扼杀美国核电的原因。
But the connection to nuclear plants, it's not complete because protests are not what killed American nuclear power.

461
00:31:16,420 --> 00:31:21,640
Three Mile Island 的事故可能是迄今为止最严重的核反应堆事故。
Breakdown in an atomic power plant is probably the worst nuclear reactor accident to date.

462
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:27,700
今天从 Three Mile Island 核电站意外释放出的更多辐射导致了一系列后果。
An unexpected release of more radiation today from the Three Mile Island nuclear power plant has led to a series of consequences.

463
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:31,840
在 Three Mile Island 事件之后，所有正在建设中的电厂都必须在建设中途重新设计。
After Three Mile Island, every plant under construction had to be redesigned, mid-built.

464
00:31:32,100 --> 00:31:33,420
成本翻了一倍，然后又翻了三倍。
Costs doubled, then they tripled.

465
00:31:33,712 --> 00:31:38,432
举个例子，在华盛顿州，一组公共事业公司同时启动了五个电厂。
As just one example, in Washington state, a group of public utilities had started five plants at once.

466
00:31:38,592 --> 00:31:42,672
当资金耗尽时，他们违约了，那笔债务按今天的标准计算几乎高达 100 亿美元。
And when the money ran out, they defaulted on what today would be almost $10 billion in debt.

467
00:31:42,862 --> 00:31:45,152
这是美国历史上规模最大的公共事业公司违约案。
It's the largest utility default in American history.

468
00:31:45,326 --> 00:31:47,716
而在全国各地，另外一百多个电厂被取消了。
And around the country, another hundred plants were canceled.

469
00:31:48,046 --> 00:31:53,188
当项目搞砸了的时候，普通人，也就是普通的用电客户，往往得承担账单。
And when the projects went bad, regular people, regular electricity customers, were often left holding the bill.

470
00:31:53,472 --> 00:31:55,952
所以 30 年来，没人动工建设新的电厂。
So for 30 years, nobody broke ground on a new one.

471
00:31:56,112 --> 00:31:59,392
没人有足够的财力来支撑成本的上涨。
Nobody had pockets deep enough to backstop the cost increases.

472
00:31:59,632 --> 00:32:04,352
反正人们本来就不想在附近建这种东西，现在还有很大可能变成他们得付钱的账单？
Something people didn't want near them anyway, that had a high chance of becoming a bill they would pay for?

473
00:32:04,592 --> 00:32:05,316
根本没戏。
Non-starter.

474
00:32:05,710 --> 00:32:06,912
但州际公路就不一样了。
But the interstates.

475
00:32:07,152 --> 00:32:11,792
州际公路走上另一条路，原因与它们是否受欢迎无关。
The interstates went the other way for a reason that had nothing to do with them being popular.

476
00:32:12,032 --> 00:32:16,032
简单来说，联邦政府支付了每美元 90 美分的建设费用。
Quite simply, the federal government paid 90 cents of every dollar to construct them.

477
00:32:16,192 --> 00:32:24,592
冲突是真实的，有些高速公路被叫停了，整个社区被保住了，
但争论其实只是关于路怎么走，从来不是关于谁来付账单。
The fights were real, some highways got stopped, whole neighborhoods got saved, but the fights were only really ever about where the
road went, never about who paid for the bill.

478
00:32:24,942 --> 00:32:27,108
这是一场混乱的斗争，但是一场可以承受的斗争。
It's a messy fight, but a fight that was survivable.

479
00:32:27,598 --> 00:32:33,152
今天，现在，就在这整件事刚开始三年，所有人都在密切关注账单。
Today, now, just three years into this whole thing, everybody is intently watching the bill.

480
00:32:33,392 --> 00:32:39,472
州长凯西·霍克尔在 7 月中旬签署了一项为期 12 个月的暂停令，停止数据 AI 数据中心的开发。
A 12-month moratorium was signed in mid-July by Governor Kathy Hochul, halting the development of data AI centers.

481
00:32:39,632 --> 00:32:43,312
州长格雷格·阿博特正在暂停整个 Texas 的数据中心许可证。
Governor Greg Abbott is pausing all data center permits across Texas.

482
00:32:43,552 --> 00:32:46,352
这些公告，如果你称之为禁令，那也太牵强了。
These announcements, you would be stretching to call these bans.

483
00:32:46,512 --> 00:32:49,392
按任何定义，这里用禁令都不是正确的词。
By any definition, ban isn't really the right word here.

484
00:32:49,552 --> 00:32:54,948
这些是纸面文件，意思是如果你想建数据中心，你必须证明你能付得起钱，尤其是电费。
These are pieces of paper saying if you want to build a data center, you have to prove you can pay for it, especially the electricity.

485
00:32:55,246 --> 00:32:55,792
Ohio。
Ohio.

486
00:32:56,142 --> 00:33:00,004
如果你预留了大量的电量却没出现，你仍然要承担 85% 的成本。
Reserve enormous amounts of electricity and don't show up, you still owe 85% of the cost.

487
00:33:00,206 --> 00:33:04,352
在 Virginia，数据中心正被划分到独立的客户类别，拥有自己的费率。
Virginia, data centers are being separated into their own customer class with their own rates.

488
00:33:04,558 --> 00:33:05,252
路易斯安那州。
Louisiana.

489
00:33:05,934 --> 00:33:10,592
为了一个 Meta 数据中心，新建了三座燃气发电厂，Meta 必须支付费用，还有宾夕法尼亚州。
Three new gas plants for one Meta data center, which Meta has to pay for, and Pennsylvania.

490
00:33:12,302 --> 00:33:17,668
有一家公司同意从一座核电站购买 20 年的电力，以便其所有者重新启动它。
A company agreed to buy power from a nuclear plant for 20 years so its owner would restart it.

491
00:33:17,904 --> 00:33:19,904
那座电厂就是 Three Mile Island。
That plant is Three Mile Island.

492
00:33:20,144 --> 00:33:26,704
Constellation Energy 表示，它将重新启用 Three Mile Island 核电站，
并将所有电力出售给 Microsoft。
Constellation Energy said that it will reopen the Three Mile Island nuclear power plant, selling all of the power to Microsoft.

493
00:33:26,864 --> 00:33:34,436
那座曾给美国核电业钉上最后一颗棺材钉的建筑，现在正
为了一个比核电站还不够受欢迎的数据中心而重新启动。
The building that put the final nail in the coffin of American nuclear power is being restarted for a data center,
a thing that is less popular than nuclear plants.

494
00:33:35,150 --> 00:33:37,188
这一次，客户付得起账单。
This time, the customer can afford the bill.

495
00:33:37,518 --> 00:33:42,820
在上次大规模建设中，当成本飙升时，那是由普通民众、普通的用电客户来承担的。
In the last great build-out, when costs exploded, it would land on regular people, ordinary electric customers.

496
00:33:43,406 --> 00:33:47,584
今天，没人真的确切知道 AI 最终会如何演变。
Today, nobody really knows exactly how AI plays out.

497
00:33:47,744 --> 00:33:56,740
任何说自己知道的人都在推销产品，但数据中心的争端可
能，甚至很可能是一个关于更古老、更简单问题的争端。
Anybody who says they do is selling something, but the data center fight could be, might be,
maybe even likely is a fight over a much older and a much simpler question.

498
00:33:56,944 --> 00:33:59,904
当成本落地时，谁在承受它？
When the cost lands, who is sitting underneath it?

499
00:34:00,174 --> 00:34:03,104
是那座只有一千人的小镇，还是建造那个“盒子”的人？
The town of a thousand people or whoever built the box?

500
00:34:03,278 --> 00:34:07,172
其实还有其他我想聊的内容，比如汽车工厂与数据中心的对比。
There are actually other things I'd like to go on about, like car factories versus data centers.

501
00:34:07,344 --> 00:34:12,835
人们接受工厂，因为工厂能提供就业，但数据中心就不太一样，还有其他好几件事。
People accept factories, they come with jobs, data centers not so much, and about half a dozen other things.

502
00:34:13,264 --> 00:34:14,864
但我已经严重超时了。
But I am way over time.

503
00:34:15,024 --> 00:34:16,164
我得结束了。
I have to end this.

504
00:34:16,384 --> 00:34:17,184
所以谁知道呢？
So who knows?

505
00:34:17,264 --> 00:34:18,704
也许这件事还有另一个角度。
Maybe there's another angle to this whole thing.

506
00:34:18,784 --> 00:34:19,664
我们下次再探讨。
We'll explore another time.

507
00:34:19,744 --> 00:34:21,984
但在那之前，感谢您的观看。
But until that time, thank you for watching.

508
00:34:22,158 --> 00:34:23,824
噢，还有，点个订阅按钮。
Oh, and hit that subscribe button.

509
00:34:23,904 --> 00:34:25,024
它就在下面某个地方。
It is down there somewhere.

510
00:34:25,326 --> 00:34:26,500
我们下个视频再见。
And I will see you in the next video.

511
00:34:26,926 --> 00:34:27,199
再见。
See you.

